# Fields

A field is a small fact your app keeps about a user or a
[group](/docs/auth/groups): a username, a display name, a plan, a billing
id. Fields save you a table and a migration for the things nearly every app
wants.

## Declaring a field

Declare a field once, as a function, and use that declaration to write and
to read. The two can then never disagree about the name or the type:

```gleam
import gleam/string
import howdy/auth/field

pub fn username() -> field.Field(String) {
  field.text("username")
  |> field.unique
  |> field.check(fn(name) {
    case string.length(name) >= 3 {
      True -> Ok(Nil)
      False -> Error("must be at least 3 characters")
    }
  })
}

pub fn seats() -> field.Field(Int) {
  field.int("seats")
}
```

The kinds of field:

- `field.text`, `field.int` and `field.bool`
- `field.time` holds a `gleam/time` `Timestamp`
- `field.custom(name, encode:, decode:)` holds a type of your own, stored
  as text

And what can be added to one:

- `field.unique`: only one holder of each value in the whole installation.
- `field.unique_in_group`: only one holder of each value within a group.
  For user fields only.
- `field.check(with:)`: refuse some values. The message comes back as a
  `400`, after the field's name: `username: must be at least 3 characters`.

A name is 1 to 64 lowercase letters, digits and underscores. User fields
and group fields are named separately, so both can have a `plan`. A value
is at most 1024 bytes once encoded.

## Writing and reading

```gleam
import howdy/auth/user
import howdy/auth/users

// Write. All of the changes apply, or none.
let assert Ok(_) =
  users.update(
    identity,
    principal.user.id,
    [field.set(username(), "ada")],
    by: user.Acting(principal),
  )

// Read.
use data <- result.try(users.fields(identity, principal.user.id))
let name = field.get(data, username()) |> result.unwrap(principal.user.email)

// Find the users holding a value.
let assert Ok([ada]) = users.find(identity, where: username(), is: "ada")
```

`field.clear(username())` is the change that removes a value.
`groups.update`, `groups.fields` and `groups.find` do the same for groups.

To set fields in the same transaction that creates the row, use
`auth.provision_with(identity, email, fields:, by:)` and
`groups.create_with(identity, id:, name:, fields:, by:)`.

Fields are not loaded when a request is authenticated, so `ctx.guard.user`
does not carry them. Call `users.fields` where you need them.

`users.get`, `users.fields` and `users.find` called through
[`auth.in_group`](/docs/auth/groups) only see that group's users.

Like the rest of user administration, these functions are privileged and
are not exposed over HTTP. A handler that lets a visitor change their own
username is fine; one that takes a user id from the request must first check
the caller may act on that user.

## Uniqueness

The database enforces uniqueness, so a taken value is a `409` even when two
requests race.

Declare a field unique before it is first written. Values stored while it
was not unique are not checked against.

Values that are unique within a group follow their user through
`groups.move`, which is a `Conflict` if the destination already holds one.

## Audit

A change is recorded as `user.fields_changed` or `group.fields_changed`,
with the names of the fields. The values never reach the
[audit trail](/docs/auth/administration).

## Timestamps

`user.User` and `group.Group` carry `created_at` and `updated_at` as
`gleam/time` timestamps, in whole seconds. Their JSON has them as RFC 3339
strings.

A user's `updated_at` moves when they are suspended or resumed, change
group, or have a field changed. A group's moves when it is renamed or has a
field changed. Passwords, sessions and membership do not move either.

## When to use your own table

Fields suit a handful of small values you look up by exact match. Anything
relational, anything large, and anything you query by range belongs in a
table of your own, with a column that references `howdy_auth_users(id)`.
[Migrations](/docs/auth/migrations) shows how to create one alongside the
auth tables.
